    text: "📖SEEDANCE 2.0 4K costs $10-12 for a 15-second clip — Is it actually worth it? One creator took the exact same reference image and prompt, then rendered it in 720p, 1080p, and 4K to find out. Here’s the real pricing breakdown for one 15-second clip: • 720p → ~68 credits • 1080p → ~135 credits • 4K → ~330 credits (~$10-12) The price jump is massive. But is the quality jump worth it? What the test actually showed: ✅720p — Fine for simple, slow shots, but details fall apart fast in close-ups and textures. ✅1080p — The sweet spot. Most people called it “totally worth the credits.” ✅4K — Clearly wins on texture-heavy scenes (fur, water droplets, skin, metal). The difference is obvious when you zoom in and compare side-by-side. Bottom line: • For client work and final delivery → 4K is worth it. • For testing, iteration, and most social content → 1080p gives you 90% of the result for a third of the price (and you can generate 2-3 versions instead of one). Biggest lesson: Your reference image quality matters way more than the resolution slider. A weak reference kills every version. (Seedance 2.5 isn’t out yet, but this pricing pattern is worth watching.) Want the exact prompt structure that delivered the sharpest results across all resolutions? 📥Tomorrow I want to share something more interesting with you 🔖In the article below, I described a guide to using Dreamina",
